<section image-layout="blank" id="eegalualJs" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="QPZ7Pvyh9N" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><p role="eyebrow">Visual communication · Media evolution</p><h1 font-size="46pt">The page changed its behavior</h1><p font-size="lg">It did not disappear. It moved from fixed arrangement, to interactive surface, to variable output.</p><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><grid divider="sm palette-5" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="YQIA5sp4XB" colspan="20"><h4>01 · Fixed</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="kZ5ISl4c_c" colspan="20"><h4>02 · Linked</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="cYE5qIdntE" colspan="20"><h4>03 · Variable</h4></grid-item></grid><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="18pt"><b>Thesis:</b> media evolution reorganizes production, distribution, interaction, and authorship. It does not guarantee improvement.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="sdVUHjGz-L" padding="0" colspan="25"><grid direction="vertical" spacing="none"><grid-item id="756UDBpbrf" padding="0" rowspan="50"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/u0NlYkazBUh_CQgo9NLgg.png" saved-media-id="gq70rd11z5uaxu9" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="editorial-media-study" alt="Large-format editorial studio photograph of one page-like media mechanism evolving across three aligned stages: a rigid archive-buff paper frame, a linked screen-grey window, and several variable sheets emerging along vermilion plotter paths, arranged as a precise material study under cool raking daylight on warm paper, carbon black structural elements, generous negative space, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item><grid-item id="2GtyIQ1AfO" background-color="accent" padding="10pt 14pt" rowspan="10"><p role="caption">Conceptual media study · not archival evidence</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="reIb1gQvt_" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="8tfcHvCDtG"><p role="eyebrow">Analytical lens · System before surface</p><h2>A medium is a system</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="0OTmEEpSfq" vertical-align="center" colspan="25"><p>Tools and formats shape what can be made. Infrastructures shape circulation. Interfaces shape perception, while conventions shape participation.</p><grid divider="sm neutral-border" direction="vertical" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="MiBUoAaP_3" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>Fixed instance</b> · Print stabilizes a version.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NDwS-PiYhV" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>Coded state</b> · Digital systems update and respond.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="aZncbvht3z" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>Generated variation</b> · Computational systems generate and vary.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Transformation occurs when these relationships change—not when a surface merely looks new.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="BDqsP84Hhe" align="center" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><diagram template-key="inputs" align="center" width="500pt" height="320pt" data-meta="{&quot;general&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Medium as a system</h4>&quot;}],&quot;step&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Tools &amp; formats</h4><p>Define what can be created</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Infrastructure</h4><p>Determines how work circulates</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Interfaces</h4><p>Shape perception and access</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Conventions</h4><p>Organize participation</p>&quot;}]}"></diagram></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="GnHdTipMAC"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="z2MGcl8Abw" vertical-align="center" colspan="27"><p role="eyebrow">Print · historical evidence</p><h2>Print stabilizes space</h2><p>Movable type makes a composed page repeatable. In this Gutenberg Bible page, two columns and a strong text hierarchy organize a fixed reading surface.</p><p>The reader follows a sequence already arranged on the page. Production combines repeatable type with the material character of paper and ornament.</p><p><b>Design question:</b> what must stay stable across many copies?</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2006680004/">Library of Congress · cph.3a51868</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="fgxz_JjeMa" vertical-align="center" colspan="33"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/agent-uploads/HZgmUAeVC4pz8Wr__MP82.jpg" source="image.upload" height="320pt" alt="Gutenberg Bible page. Library of Congress, Prints &amp; Photographs Division, cph.3a51868. Historical page reproduction."><p font-size="12pt" role="caption">Gutenberg Bible page. Library of Congress, Prints &amp; Photographs Division, cph.3a51868. Historical page reproduction.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="78vwvLl0Bk"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="RifaK6NAEA" vertical-align="center" colspan="27"><p role="eyebrow">Digital · preserved interface</p><h2>The document joins a network</h2><p>CERN’s preserved early Web page makes the shift visible: linked terms turn a reading surface into a set of possible routes.</p><p>Berners-Lee proposed the Web at CERN in 1989. CERN released the Web software into the public domain on 30 April 1993.</p><p><b>Design question:</b> where can the reader go next, and how will they return?</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://info.cern.ch/hypertext/WWW/TheProject.html">CERN · preserved first website</a> · <a href="https://home.cern/science/computing/the-birth-of-the-web/">Web history</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="8g-JJXGiZP" vertical-align="center" colspan="33"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/agent-uploads/izdz8Kx9jyx_YmD5wTHlS.png" source="image.upload" height="320pt" alt="CERN-preserved early Web page, rendered in a modern browser for this prototype; not a period browser screenshot."><p font-size="12pt" role="caption">CERN-preserved early Web page, rendered in a modern browser for this prototype; not a period browser screenshot.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="57sLOYhS9H"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="5nLmNAYbYI"><p role="eyebrow">Three-era comparison · Dominant tendencies</p><h2>What changes across the three eras?</h2><table col-widths="[16,28,28,28]" header-row="true" header-col="true" borders="all" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><p>Dimension</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>Print · Fixed instance</p></td><td background-color="palette-3"><p><span text-color="#171A1C">Digital · Coded state</span></p></td><td background-color="accent"><p>Computational · Generated variation</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Production</p></td><td><p>Composed instance</p></td><td><p>Coded state</p></td><td><p>Generated variation</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Distribution</p></td><td><p>Editions and physical circulation</p></td><td><p>Network access and maintained versions</p></td><td><p>On-demand synthesis</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Audience</p></td><td><p>Reader and interpreter</p></td><td><p>User who changes system state</p></td><td><p>Participant and evaluator</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Authorship</p></td><td><p>Makers and production trades</p></td><td><p>Collaborative technical stacks</p></td><td><p>People, data, models, and interfaces</p></td></tr></tbody></table><aside icon="false"><p font-size="18pt">These are overlapping analytical lenses, not sealed eras. Each newer system carries older forms—and their assumptions—inside it.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="BEYU4ODIlj" vertical-align="start"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="ia5gtTPcKs"><p role="eyebrow">Era 03 · Generated variation</p><h2>Computational intelligence makes the output variable</h2><p font-size="18pt">The designer increasingly specifies a space of possible outputs rather than one resolved artifact.</p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="subtle" marker-shape="chevron" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-fill="true" spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug" padding="1em" style="--marker-chip-padding: 0.75em;"><grid-item id="VOnaVbBZGp" colspan="10"><h4>Intent + constraints</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="S7U2uUrte1" colspan="10"><h4>Data, rules, or prompt</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="UpPqT2yWeK" colspan="10"><h4>Model computation</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="EhpUD1aREZ" colspan="10"><h4>Output</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="0xdrqIVHgq" colspan="10"><h4>Human judgment</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="x_7A7K2-X7" colspan="10"><h4>Revise ↺</h4></grid-item></grid><p font-size="18pt"><b>The loop closes:</b> evaluation updates the next constraints; the system is iterative, not autonomous.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="dCQjsPlGmU" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow"><span text-color="#30363A">What scales</span></p><h3>Variation and adaptation</h3><p font-size="16pt">Repeated synthesis can respond to many contexts.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="n6WvFgZCVM" background-color="accent" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">What becomes design work</p><h3>Conditions for judgment</h3><p font-size="16pt">Provenance, opacity, bias, evaluation, and responsible revision.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="q-E8Z8wKWW" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="AfrZPTs4bB"><p role="eyebrow">Accumulation, not replacement</p><h2>Progress is not a staircase</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="LbFm63NsEF" background-color="palette-2" colspan="20"><h3>Print</h3><p font-size="18pt">Stable, inspectable composition. Revision is slower; circulation remains materially constrained.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FvXknDeBTR" background-color="palette-3" colspan="20"><h3>Digital</h3><p font-size="18pt">Access, interaction, and rapid updates. Dependence, volatility, and distraction enter the experience.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="lpfYeEKslh" background-color="accent" colspan="20"><h3>Computational</h3><p font-size="18pt">Variation and scale. Opacity, homogenization, and uncertain provenance complicate judgment.</p></grid-item></grid><custom-code>
<style>
  .era-overlap { width: 100%; font-family: var(--body-font); color: var(--body-color); }
  .era-overlap .overlap-title { font-family: var(--heading-font); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.35em; }
  .era-overlap .track { position: relative; height: 5em; border-left: 1px solid var(--body-color); border-right: 1px solid var(--body-color); }
  .era-overlap .bar { position: absolute; height: 1.2em; display: flex; align-items: center; padding-left: 0.75em; font-size: 0.78em; font-weight: 600; }
  .era-overlap .print { top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--palette-2) 72%, var(--card-color)); }
  .era-overlap .digital { top: 1.65em; left: 24%; width: 76%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--palette-3) 72%, var(--card-color)); }
  .era-overlap .computational { top: 3.3em; left: 52%; width: 48%; background: var(--accent-color); color: var(--card-color); }
</style>
<div class="era-overlap">
  <div class="overlap-title">Media persist inside one another</div>
  <div class="track" aria-label="Schematic overlap of print, digital, and computational media">
    <div class="bar print">Print systems continue</div>
    <div class="bar digital">Digital systems join them</div>
    <div class="bar computational">Computational systems accumulate</div>
  </div>
</div>
</custom-code><p font-size="16pt">Schematic overlap—not a chronological scale. Designers combine systems according to purpose, audience, and consequence, not novelty alone.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="RuwL2MW5Yj" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="m2L_NYyRC5" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Studio method · Closing exercise</p><h1 font-size="34pt">One message.<br>Three behaviors.<br>Five questions.</h1><p font-size="18pt">Keep the communicative intent constant. Change the medium, then document how roles and consequences change.</p><table col-widths="[39,61]" borders="rows" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding-block: 7pt; --table-cell-padding-inline: 0pt;"><tbody><tr><td><p><b>Print · Fixed</b></p></td><td><p>Resolve one stable edition.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Interactive · Linked</b></p></td><td><p>Design states and responses.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Generative · Variable</b></p></td><td><p>Bound variation and evaluate outputs.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></grid-item><grid-item id="V7pqXhck1T" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Critique before choosing the medium</p><grid divider="sm palette-5" marker="number" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" direction="vertical" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-inline-size: 32pt; --marker-gap: 14pt;"><grid-item id="YmjjUfUnn7" padding="8pt 0"><h4>What is fixed, and what can vary?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="U23H7TYI6S" padding="8pt 0"><h4>Who can make, publish, revise, and respond?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="Crkrh5ADLA" padding="8pt 0"><h4>How does the work travel?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="_KJ2PsOLlh" padding="8pt 0"><h4>Where are decisions visible or hidden?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="4j2f_RVD3P" padding="8pt 0"><h4>What does the interface ask the audience to become?</h4></grid-item></grid><spacer size="14pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="tyeykGgh8V" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><p font-size="18pt"><b>Final test:</b> if only the surface changed, the medium has not yet been redesigned.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>